#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# diagonal_matrix

#% text
# `diagonal_matrix(v)` gives the square matrix with the elements of `v` on the diagonal and zeros elsewhere.

#% text
# `diagonal_matrix(v, k)` puts `v` on the `k`-th diagonal: above the main diagonal for `k > 0`, below it for `k < 0`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The matrix has side `length(v) + abs(k)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
diagonal_matrix([1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= [[1, 0, 0], [0, 2, 0], [0, 0, 3]]

#% cell
diagonal_matrix([a, b])
#% output
#= [[a, 0], [0, b]]

#% cell
diagonal_matrix([1, 1], 1)
#% output
#= [[0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 1], [0, 0, 0]]

#% cell
diagonal_matrix([5, 6], -1)
#% output
#= [[0, 0, 0], [5, 0, 0], [0, 6, 0]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A tridiagonal matrix as a sum of three diagonals:

#% cell
diagonal_matrix([2, 2, 2]) + diagonal_matrix([-1, -1], 1) + diagonal_matrix([-1, -1], -1)
#% output
#= [[2, -1, 0], [-1, 2, -1], [0, -1, 2]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The determinant is the product of the diagonal:

#% cell
det(diagonal_matrix([4, 5, 6]))
#% output
#= 120

#% text
# The powers of a diagonal matrix are the powers of the entries:

#% cell
matrix_power(diagonal_matrix([2, 3]), 5)
#% output
#= [[32, 0], [0, 243]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `identity_matrix`, `trace`, `matrix_power`
